@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: where a secret RESTS between runs on macOS, and how it reaches a run
' covers: internal/secretref/secretref.go
' covers: internal/credentials/keychain.go
' covers: internal/credentials/credentials.go
' covers: internal/runner/runner.go
' covers: internal/backend/dockeregress/dockeregress.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go
' plan: _spec/_plans/secret-custody.puml — this is the macOS slice of it, applied
' the plan's remaining exposure (d) is blocked on _spec/_plans/retire-dind.puml:
' the blob's destination problem: _spec/internal/sbx/oauth-provisioning.puml
' it closes by REMOVING the docker backend, not by patching this layer
'
' ── MEASURED ON darwin 26.6.2 / claude 2.1.259 / sbx 0.39.0 ────────────────
' The plan was written on Linux, where there is no `security` binary and no
' Keychain, so its macOS half was inference. Four of those inferences were
' wrong. Everything below was read off this host or off the shipped binaries.
'
'   1. THE ITEM.  `security dump-keychain` reports, with NO GUI prompt:
'        svce = "Claude Code-credentials"      acct = $USER
'      The payload is raw UTF-8 JSON (509 bytes here), not hex, and `-a` is
'      NOT required — the service alone is unique.
'
'   2. THE PAYLOAD IS THE WHOLE CREDENTIAL, which is what the plan demanded
'      and could not confirm: accessToken (sk-ant-oat01-…), refreshToken
'      (sk-ant-ort01-…), expiresAt, refreshTokenExpiresAt, scopes,
'      subscriptionType. Same shape as ~/.claude/.credentials.json. So the
'      unit that can cross a boundary is the blob, never a bare token.
'
'   3. THERE IS NO DIALOG, AND NO "Always Allow" TO WARN ABOUT. The plan's
'      note said proveo's first read would prompt, and that approving it
'      would widen the ACL to /usr/bin/security. The widening is real and
'      the TIMING was wrong: it already happened at login. The item's ACL is
'
'        entry 0: authorizations decrypt derive export_clear export_wrapped
'                 applications (1): /usr/bin/security (OK)
'                 requirement: identifier "com.apple.security" and anchor apple
'
'      because `claude` stores the credential BY SHELLING OUT to
'        security add-generic-password -U -a "$acct" -s "$svc" -X "<hex>"
'      (read out of the 2.1.259 bundle). The creating app becomes the sole
'      trusted app for decrypt, so every later read through /usr/bin/security
'      by any process of that user succeeds unprompted, forever. proveo
'      inherits an ACL it did not widen. Warning "before the dialog" would
'      warn about a dialog that never appears — so what ships instead is an
'      announcement before the exec, which is true on a host where the item
'      WAS provisioned some other way and harmless on one where it was not.
'
'   4. THE SERVICE NAME IS COMPUTED, NOT GUESSED. `claude` builds it at
'      runtime; proveo reproduces the same algorithm rather than hardcoding
'      the one string this host happens to have:
'        service = "Claude Code" + OAUTH_FILE_SUFFIX + "-credentials" + hash
'        hash    = "" when the config dir is the default,
'                  else "-" + sha256(dir NFC)[:8]
'        acct    = $USER validated /^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$/, else "claude-code-user"
'      CLAUDE_SECURESTORAGE_CONFIG_DIR redirects which dir is hashed, and
'      set-but-EMPTY switches the hash OFF even when CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR names
'      one — three states, not two. Candidates are tried most-specific first
'      and a candidate is TAKEN only when its payload parses as a login with
'      a non-empty access token, so a wrong name fails rather than half-works.
'      "Claude Code" (no suffix) stays last: claude-code < 2.x wrote it, and
'      anthropics/claude-code#1470 shows it in the wild.
'
' ── THE READ INHERITS APPLE'S TRUST, AND THAT IS A CHOSEN POSTURE ─────────
' proveo shells out to /usr/bin/security, so its reads are SILENT — not because
' a permission was granted to proveo, but because the item's ACL trusts that
' binary and proveo is merely using it. Two consequences, both stated rather
' than discovered later:
'
'   proveo's read is indistinguishable from any other process of the same user
'   doing the same thing. There is no per-app consent, and no prompt to expect.
'   This is load-bearing beyond diagnosis: a ""keychain:"" reference resolves a
'   real API key that DOES reach the agent, so live secrets cross this path.
'
' THE ALTERNATIVE, PRICED AND DECLINED (2026-09-03). Calling
' SecItemCopyMatching from proveo's own process would make proveo the
' requesting app: macOS would raise the per-app dialog on first read of each
' item, and "Always Allow" would add proveo — not ""security"" — to that item's
' ACL. That is genuinely stronger custody.
'
' It costs cgo. ""\.goreleaser\.yaml"" sets ""CGO_ENABLED=0"" and cross-compiles
' darwin beside linux, freebsd and windows from one runner, so the cgo build
' needs a darwin runner or an osxcross SDK — a release-pipeline change rather
' than a code change. Declined on that basis, with the shell-out kept and the
' inherited trust written down here so the next reader knows it was weighed.
'
' The bounded exec and the Announce hook stay either way: they exist for the
' host where an item was provisioned by some OTHER app, where /usr/bin/security
' is NOT in the ACL and the dialog does appear.
'
' ── WHY THE CREDENTIAL IS READ, REPORTED, AND DROPPED ──────────────────────
' The plan routed the blob to a backend. Measured, neither backend can take
' it, and the two fail differently:
'
'   docker   the agent's credential IS ~/.claude/.credentials.json in the
'            mounted proveo home. Writing it there puts a plaintext token in
'            a bind-mounted directory readable by every later run that mounts
'            it, outliving the run that made it — exposure (d) in its worst
'            shape, which the plan refuses outright.
'   sbx      sbx's own secret store IS the macOS Keychain (service group
'            com.docker.sandboxes; entries at
'            docker/sandbox/credentials/_/NAME and
'            docker/sandbox/default/anthropic). Its built-in anthropic
'            service already declares an oauth credential whose proxy
'            materialises ~/.claude/.credentials.json INSIDE the sandbox from
'            a template, with proxy-managed sentinels
'            (sk-ant-oat01-proxy-managed / sk-ant-ort01-proxy-managed) and a
'            tokenEndpoint at platform.claude.com/v1/oauth/token. The real
'            tokens never enter the sandbox. That is exactly the custody the
'            plan wanted — and `sbx secret set --oauth` is documented
'            openai/global only, so proveo has NO supported way to write that
'            slot. `sbx secret set CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` with the blob
'            would store a JSON object where sbx expects a bearer token and
'            its proxy would inject the object as a header.
'
' So the value proveo adds on macOS is the DIAGNOSIS, which was the actual
' gap: proveo could see a blanked file and a working `claude` and had no way
' to say which credential existed or where. The blob is parsed for its
' metadata and discarded; KeychainLogin holds no token, and a test asserts
' that over the rendered struct so a field added later cannot retain one.
'
' ── THE INVERSION THIS MUST NOT TRIP ───────────────────────────────────────
' The host store feeds NO decision. Letting it reach HasPersistedLogin or
' AuthSuppressor would, on sbx: flip PersistedLogin true, drop the anthropic
' env vars, skip addSecret — and hand the sandbox NOTHING, while a Keychain
' proveo cannot deliver from sat there looking like an answer. That is the
' sharpest hazard in the design and read-and-report is what stays clear of it.
' Pinned by TestKeychainNeverFeedsSuppression.
'
' ── WHY IT IS READ ON EVERY darwin RUN ─────────────────────────────────────
' Not gated on the proveo home's file looking blanked. Gating inverts the
' authority: the Keychain is where `claude` on macOS KEEPS the credential, so
' the file is the derived artefact. A file can be absent, stale, or shaped in
' a way the parser does not recognise for reasons that have nothing to do
' with the store — each of which reads as "no login" while a live one sits
' unread. Skipped on --print alone, because a run that only renders argv must
' not ask the operator for anything.
'
' ── EXPOSURE (c): "IN MEMORY" WAS AN OVERSTATEMENT ─────────────────────────
' A bare `-e NAME` keeps the value out of argv, which is what closed (c) —
' `ps` sees a name and no value. But HydrateProcessEnv called os.Setenv, so
' the value entered proveo's OWN environ: readable in /proc/<pid>/environ for
' the life of the process, and inherited by every child spawned afterwards —
' docker, git, sbx, the browser relay, an operator's hook.
'
' ChildEnv replaces it. Both backends copy a bare name out of the launching
' process's environment, so the value only has to be in the environment of
' the ONE exec that forwards it. cmd.Env on that exec costs a field on two
' call sites; DockerRunArgs never reads it, so --print renders the same argv
' whether or not a reference resolved.
'
' ── THE LINUX HALF IS NOT DONE HERE ────────────────────────────────────────
' cmd: and pass: work everywhere and keychain: answers Unsupported off darwin,
' naming the escape hatch rather than shelling out to a binary that is not
' there. The plan's Linux ranking (systemd-creds, pass, Secret Service, cmd:,
' keyctl, age/sops) stands unimplemented; secret-service: is the obvious next
' resolver and is deliberately absent rather than guessed at.
title secret references — where a secret rests, and how it reaches one run

actor "operator" as OP

node "host secret store\nproveo adopts one, never becomes one" as STORE <<host>> {
  component "darwin: login Keychain\n""security find-generic-password -w -s""" as KC <<app>>
  component "any store, via a command\n1Password · Bitwarden · Vault · keepassxc" as CMD <<app>>
  component "pass (GPG)" as PASS <<app>>
}

component "internal/secretref\nParse · Resolve · one bounded exec" as REF <<app>>
component "internal/credentials\nProviderLookup — the ONLY credential read" as LOOK <<app>>
component "credentials.ChildEnv\nvalues for ONE exec's cmd.Env" as CHILD <<app>>
component "KeychainLogin\nmetadata only — the blob is dropped" as KLOGIN <<app>>

component "docker run / sbx run\nbare ""-e NAME"" — argv carries the NAME only" as LAUNCH <<app>>
component "the agent" as AGENT <<app>>

component "plaintext in .env,\nor exported in a shell rc" as REST <<error>>
component "proveo's own environ\n/proc/<pid>/environ, and every later child" as ENVIRON <<error>>
component "a token written into\nthe mounted proveo home" as HOMEFILE <<error>>

OP ARROW_MAIN KC : logs in once, with ""claude""
REF ARROW_MAIN KC : read at RUN TIME, never written
REF ARROW_MAIN CMD
REF ARROW_MAIN PASS
LOOK ARROW_MAIN REF : only when the value parses as a reference
LOOK ARROW_MAIN CHILD
CHILD ARROW_MAIN LAUNCH : " ""cmd.Env"" on the one exec "
LAUNCH ARROW_MAIN AGENT
KC ARROW_MAIN KLOGIN : whole credential in, metadata out
KLOGIN ARROW_OPTIONAL OP : reports what exists, and which backend can reach it

REF ARROW_ERROR REST : what a store replaces
CHILD ARROW_ERROR ENVIRON : what ""cmd.Env"" replaces
KLOGIN ARROW_ERROR HOMEFILE : refused outright

note bottom of REF
  **Every failure is a warning and a no-op.**
  No file, no override, no suppression change,
  and never an error carrying the value it was
  handling. A denial must not leave the run
  worse than not having asked.
end note

note bottom of REF
  **The bound is mandatory, and paired with an
  announcement rather than traded against it.**
  A tight bound loses a race the operator was
  never told about; an unbounded one freezes
  proveo before launch with a blank screen.
end note

note top of REF
  **Unknown schemes stay literal.** A value with
  no scheme this package knows is a secret, so
  a key holding a colon is never mistaken for
  one, and nothing breaks by not adopting it.
  ""env:"" and "".env"" survive as resolvers.
end note

note bottom of KC
  **The ACL was already this wide.** ""claude""
  created the item through ""/usr/bin/security"",
  so that binary is the sole trusted app for
  decrypt and proveo's read is silent. proveo
  inherits the widening; it does not cause it.
end note

note bottom of CMD
  **The honest escape hatch.** Linux has no one
  Keychain, so a resolver running the operator's
  own command covers every manager without
  proveo integrating with any of them.
end note

note bottom of CMD
  **On macOS it is the KeePassXC answer too.**
  KeePassXC speaks Secret Service on Linux
  only, so ""cmd:keepassxc-cli"" is the door
  there — or copy the keys into the Keychain.
end note

note bottom of KLOGIN
  **Report and reach, always together.** "There
  is a login in your Keychain" reads as "so the
  run is authenticated", which is false on both
  backends — the half-truth this area produces.
end note

note top of KLOGIN
  **It decides nothing.** Reaching
  ""HasPersistedLogin"" would, on sbx, drop the
  anthropic env vars and hand the sandbox nothing.
  Read-and-report is what stays clear of it.
end note

note bottom of CHILD
  **A name already in proveo's environ needs no
  pair.** The child inherits it anyway, so
  restating it would put the value somewhere it
  already was. Empty ChildEnv leaves ""cmd.Env""
  nil and os/exec passes the environment through.
end note

legend bottom
  **The four exposures, and what this slice does to each**
  ==
  **(a) at rest** — CLOSED on macOS. ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=keychain:"" resolves from the
  store the operator already keeps, so no plaintext has to be written anywhere.
  --
  **(b) held by the agent** — unchanged. The broker still owns this, and stays the default.
  --
  **(c) in argv** — was already closed by the bare ""-e NAME""; its NEIGHBOUR is closed
  now. The value reaches one child's ""cmd.Env"" instead of proveo's own environ.
  --
  **(d) in a mounted file** — STILL OPEN on the docker path, and BLOCKED rather than
  merely undone. ""WriteBrokerEnv"" puts every provider key as plaintext in ""broker.env""
  and mounts it into the proxy: 0600, per-run, outside every agent mount, but disk.
  ""broker.Route.Value"" already outranks ""ValueFile"", so the mechanism exists — yet the
  exposure that matters is the agent's own "".credentials.json"" in the mounted home, and
  that one closes only when the docker backend GOES — which retiring the privileged
  sidecar did not do: it kept docker+egress as the ""PROVEO_SBX=0"" fallback on purpose
  (_spec/_plans/retire-dind\.puml), so (d) waits on deleting the backend, not the sidecar.
  Exercising the broker half here would also need ""proveo build egress-proxy"" to land.
  ==
  **What macOS cannot settle either.** A denied dialog and a no-GUI read were not
  measured — provoking the first needs a modal on the operator's screen. The classifier
  therefore keys on what IS measured (exit 44, "could not be found") plus the documented
  interaction wording, and QUOTES ""security"" verbatim for everything else rather than
  labelling it. A wrong label sends the operator to the wrong fix; a quote does not.
end legend
@enduml
